questions.question:
Given a 128-bit header split into hi and lo, pack four fields into non-overlapping bit ranges: A (bits 0-7), B (bits 8-19), C (bits 32-47) in lo, and D (bits 80-111) in hi. Implement:

void packHeader128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint8_t A, uint16_t B, uint16_t C, uint32_t D, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo);

Use only bitwise operations (shifts, AND, OR, NOT); no loops or conditionals. Provide the function signature, a brief justification, plus 1-2 quick examples?

questions.explanation:
## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of 128-bit bit packing, non-overlapping fields, and in-place updates without loops. It exercises careful mask calculation and cross-boundary writes.

## Key Concepts
- Bit masks and clearing specific ranges
- Cross-boundary placement across lo/hi
- Safe in-place updates without branching
- Endianness-agnostic bit numbering

## Code Example
```c
void packHeader128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint8_t A, uint16_t B, uint16_t C, uint32_t D, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  uint64_t lo_mask = ((uint64_t)0xFF) | (((uint64_t)0xFFF) << 8) | (((uint64_t)0xFFFF) << 32);
  uint64_t hi_mask = ((uint64_t)0xFFFFFFFF) << 16;
  *out_lo = (lo & ~lo_mask) | (uint64_t)A | (((uint64_t)B) << 8) | (((uint64_t)C) << 32);
  *out_hi = (hi & ~hi_mask) | (((uint64_t)D) << 16);
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if ranges could overlap and you needed deterministic behavior?
- How would you validate correctness across all inputs in a unit test?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of advanced bit-twiddling for permutation of set bits. Evaluates ability to craft constant-time logic without loops and to reason about edge cases of the last permutation.

## Key Concepts

- Next higher permutation with fixed popcount
- Edge-case handling (max permutation -> 0)
- Pure bitwise operations and shifts; no loops or branches
- Efficient 64-bit handling and overflow behavior

## Code Example

```javascript
uint64_t nextBitPermutation64(uint64_t x) {
  uint64_t smallest = x & -x;
  uint64_t ripple = x + smallest;
  uint64_t ones = ripple & -ripple;
  uint64_t next = ripple | (((ones ^ ripple) >> 2) / smallest);
  return next;
}
```

## Examples

- `nextBitPermutation64(0b0011)` returns `0b0101` (next permutation with 2 set bits)
- `nextBitPermutation64(0b1100)` returns `0b10000` (next permutation with 2 set bits)
- `nextBitPermutation64(0b111000)` returns `0b1000011` (next permutation with 3 set bits)

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to scale classic SWAR nibble-swaps to 128-bit values, ensuring implementations stay constant-time and border-safe.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit lane-wise bit-twiddling
- SWAR-style mask-and-shift patterns
- Safe handling of hi/lo halves

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 nibbleSwap128(uint128 x) {
  const uint64_t M = 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = ((x.lo & M) << 4) | ((x.lo >> 4) & M);
  r.hi = ((x.hi & M) << 4) | ((x.hi >> 4) & M);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to 256-bit types? 
- How do you test with all-zero and all-0xFF inputs?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to derive bit-run counts without loops, handling 128-bit arithmetic and portable popcounts.

## Key Concepts

- Detect run starts with mask m = x & ~(x << 1).
- Implement 128-bit left shift across hi/lo: (hi,lo) << 1 becomes (hi<<1 | lo>>63, lo<<1).
- Compute popcount on a 128-bit value by summing 64-bit SWAR popcounts on hi and lo.

## Code Example

```javascript
// C-like pseudocode
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint128 shl1(uint128 x){
  uint128 r; r.hi = (x.hi << 1) | (x.lo >> 63); r.lo = x.lo << 1; return r; }
static inline unsigned popcount64(uint64_t x){
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56; }
unsigned countRuns128(uint128 x){
  uint128 t = shl1(x);
  uint128 m; m.hi = x.hi & ~t.hi; m.lo = x.lo & ~t.lo;
  return popcount64(m.hi) + popcount64(m.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you leverage hardware intrinsics for popcount on supported platforms?
- How would you scale the approach to 256-bit or 512-bit widths?

